    {
      "duration_ms": 12000,
      "caption": "Why it is serializable: lock order makes the conflict graph acyclic.",
      "narration": "If two transactions conflict, the one that locked first finishes with it first. So conflict edges follow lock-point order, the graph has no cycles, and a serial order exists.",
      "elements": [
        {
          "id": "h",
          "type": "title",
          "text": "Why serializable",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 6,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 0,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "l",
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "Conflicts ordered by lock acquisition",
            "Precedence graph has no cycle",
            "Equivalent to a serial order"
          ],
          "x": 8,
          "y": 22,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 500,
          "enter": "slide-up"
        },
        {
          "id": "n",
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Phantoms need range or predicate locks.",
          "x": 8,
          "y": 60,
          "w": 84,
          "at": 5000,
          "enter": "fade"
        },
        {
          "id": "f",
          "type": "highlight",
          "target": "l",
          "at": 7000,
          "enter": "fade"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "duration_ms": 10000,
      "caption": "Waiting can form a cycle: T1 waits for T2 and T2 waits for T1.",
      "narration": "T1 holds a lock on A and wants B. T2 holds B and wants A. Neither can proceed, which is a deadlock.",
